  "nameStory": "Nason's Mining Bee carries the name of its discoverer or an early researcher who studied this species. The \"mining\" part of its name comes from the female bee's habit of digging tunnels in the ground to create underground nests for her eggs. She literally mines into soil like a tiny excavator, creating branching galleries where she'll provision each chamber with pollen and nectar.",
  "description": "Nason's Mining Bee is a medium-sized solitary bee that looks somewhat like a small, fuzzy honey bee but with a more robust build. These industrious insects are excellent pollinators that visit flowers of many different plant species, making them generalist foragers. The females are ground-nesters, digging individual burrows in soil where they create underground nurseries for their young. Like other mining bees, they're gentle and rarely sting unless directly handled.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Ground nesting behavior",
      "description": "Look for small mounds of loose soil with pencil-sized holes, often in bare or sparsely vegetated areas where females dig their tunnel systems",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Fuzzy body texture",
      "description": "Body covered in dense, soft hairs that feel velvety if you gently touch a specimen - much fuzzier than a typical honey bee",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Flower visiting pattern",
      "description": "Visits many different types of flowers throughout the day, often working methodically from bloom to bloom rather than focusing on just one plant species",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Size and build",
      "description": "Medium-sized with a stocky, robust appearance - noticeably more compact and muscular-looking than honey bees",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Flight pattern",
      "description": "Flies with a somewhat buzzy, direct flight pattern, often hovering briefly before landing on flowers",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found in various habitats across New England including gardens, meadows, forest edges, and disturbed areas with exposed soil for nesting. Prefers areas with well-drained soil that's easy to excavate, often choosing south-facing slopes or areas with morning sun exposure. Look for them in places where diverse flowering plants grow near suitable nesting sites.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "Adults emerge and become active, with females beginning to excavate nests in suitable soil. Peak activity often coincides with early spring wildflowers and fruit tree blossoms.",
    "summer": "Most active period for foraging and provisioning nests. Look for fresh soil mounds and busy bees visiting a wide variety of summer flowers throughout the day.",
    "fall": "Activity winds down as the season progresses, with new generation developing underground. Adults become less common as temperatures drop.",
    "winter": "Inactive, with next generation overwintering as larvae or pupae in underground cells. No above-ground activity during cold months."
  },
  "funFact": "Each female Nason's Mining Bee is essentially a single mom running her own underground apartment complex - she digs all the tunnels, builds all the nursery chambers, collects all the food, and provisions each room with a perfectly measured ball of pollen and nectar before laying a single egg and sealing it up, never to see her offspring!",
  "confusionNotes": "Most easily confused with other mining bee species in the same genus, but the ground-nesting behavior and small soil mounds help distinguish all mining bees from honey bees or bumblebees. Remember: if you see small dirt piles with holes and fuzzy bees nearby, think \"mining bee\" rather than social bee species.",
  "contextualKnowledge": "Despite being called \"mining\" bees, these insects are beneficial pollinators, not pests. The small holes and soil mounds they create are temporary and actually help aerate soil. Unlike honey bees which live in large colonies, mining bees are solitary - each female works alone to raise her own offspring.",
  "compareHints": "Nason's Mining Bee is part of a large genus with many similar-looking relatives in New England, making species-level identification challenging without close examination by experts. Most mining bees in the Andrena genus share the ground-nesting behavior and fuzzy appearance, but they often specialize on different flowers or emerge at different times of year. The generalist feeding behavior of Nason's Mining Bee, visiting many flower types, can help distinguish it from more specialized relatives that focus on specific plant families.",
